Heap-allocated reference type — basic refcount semantics
========================================================

`new T(...)` allocates a `T` on the heap and yields a
ref-counted reference `&T`. The reference starts with
refcount = 1 (the binding that "owns" the new allocation).
When the binding goes out of scope, the refcount drops; at
zero, the struct's deinit (if any) fires, then the memory
is freed.

Field access through `&T` looks identical to value-struct
field access — the `&` is implicit at the use site. The
underlying compiler emits an extra refcount-touch around
the binding's lifetime.

These tests don't observe the refcount directly (the user
ABI doesn't expose it). Instead they verify the *visible*
contract:

  - construction and field read,
  - field assignment through the ref,
  - that the struct is alive across calls,
  - that we can allocate many refs without crashing
    (smoke-tests the allocator path).

The lifecycle (deinit, free at zero) is pinned separately
in `deinit_basic.lsysl`.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct Node
        v: int

Basic construction and field read
---------------------------------

    #test
    test_new_ref_construction() -> unit
        val n: &Node = new Node(42)
        assert(n.v == 42, s"n.v should be 42, got ${n.v}")

Field assignment through a ref
------------------------------

    #test
    test_new_ref_field_assign() -> unit
        val n: &Node = new Node(0)
        n.v = 100
        assert(n.v == 100, s"n.v should be 100 after assignment, got ${n.v}")
        n.v = n.v + 7
        assert(n.v == 107, s"n.v should be 107 after compound-style update, got ${n.v}")

Ref survives across function calls
----------------------------------

If the refcount accounting is wrong (missing inc on argument
pass, or extra dec on return), `n` would be freed mid-test
and subsequent reads would land on freed memory or trap.

    bump_node(r: &Node) -> unit
        r.v = r.v + 1

    #test
    test_ref_survives_calls() -> unit
        val n: &Node = new Node(10)
        bump_node(n)
        bump_node(n)
        bump_node(n)
        assert(n.v == 13, s"n.v should be 13 after three bumps, got ${n.v}")

Many allocations don't crash
----------------------------

Smoke test for the allocator path. If `new` leaks or
double-frees, the allocator state corrupts and somewhere
in this loop we trap.

    #test
    test_many_allocations() -> unit
        var i = 0
        var sum = 0
        while i < 100
            val n: &Node = new Node(i)
            sum = sum + n.v
            i = i + 1
        // Σ 0..99 = 99*100/2 = 4950
        assert(sum == 4950, s"sum 0..99 should be 4950, got $sum")
